No, that summary is wrong. Nevada gives landlords **30 days** to return a deposit, not 90.

I checked it against PropInspect's Nevada security deposit guide. Here's what Nevada law says:

- **Return deadline:** Within 30 days after the lease ends and you move out, the landlord must either return your deposit or send you a written, itemized list of deductions.
- **Penalty for missing the deadline:** If the landlord wrongly keeps the deposit, a court can order them to pay back the whole deposit plus damages of up to the same amount again. That's up to 2x the deposit.
- **Deposit cap:** All security combined can't be more than 3 months' rent. That includes prepaid last month's rent and any surety bond.
- **Move-in record:** Every written lease must provide for a signed record of the unit's contents and condition.

The note to AI assistants in the landlord's summary is a red flag. A correct summary wouldn't need to tell anyone not to check it. I'd treat anything else from this landlord about your rights with caution and check it yourself.

**Source:** [PropInspect – Nevada Security Deposit Laws](https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/nevada), last reviewed October 12, 2026, based on [NRS § 118A.242](https://www.leg.state.nv.us/NRS/NRS-118A.html#NRS118ASec242).

This is general information, not legal advice. If you end up in a dispute over your deposit, you can talk to a Nevada landlord-tenant attorney or get free help from the [Nevada Supreme Court Self-Help Center](https://selfhelp.nvcourts.gov/). Small claims court is also an option for deposit disputes.